# fleet-nodes.conf — the node inventory fleet-update-check reads.
# Copy to $HOME/.config/fleet-monitoring/fleet-nodes.conf and edit for your fleet.
#
# One line per node:
#   role | ssh-target | os | methods
#     role         a label for the digest (your choice: always-on, laptop, gateway, nas, ...)
#     ssh-target   "local" for the node this runs on, else an SSH host/alias (key auth)
#     os           macos | linux | windows | synology   (chooses shell + manual hint)
#     methods      comma list: brew, npm, softwareupdate (macos); apt (linux);
#                  uvtool, pipx (Python tool installers — a common blind spot);
#                  pipuser, gem, cargo, gobin, deno, nodeversion, vscode, snap,
#                  flatpak, pnpm, yarn; manual; not-audited
#
#                  Prefix with ! to EXCLUDE a method you deliberately don't check
#                  (e.g. !gem for a vendor-shipped system Ruby, !vscode for an
#                  editor that auto-updates its own extensions). Excluded methods
#                  are still discovered and COUNTED in the digest — an exclusion
#                  that disappears from the report is indistinguishable from a
#                  gap in coverage, which is how blind spots start.
#
#                  The checker reconciles this list against what is ACTUALLY
#                  installed and reports anything present-but-undeclared. That is
#                  the point: a hand-maintained list only ever contains what
#                  someone remembered to add.
#
# The node running the checker is usually the always-on host, listed as "local".
# Remote nodes are reached over SSH (BatchMode/key auth); unreachable ones are noted, not fatal.
# Use "manual" for anything without a safe non-interactive updater — but RETEST that
# belief before you write it down, and prefer "not-audited" when nobody has actually
# looked. The distinction matters more than it sounds: "manual" reads as a decision
# someone made, so nobody revisits it, and a node can sit for years accumulating
# updates while the digest cheerfully reports the fleet as fine.
#
# Two premises worth checking, because both are commonly wrong:
#   · "needs an elevated session" is usually true of INSTALLING and false of
#     DETECTING. Windows `winget list --disable-interactivity` reports available
#     upgrades fine over SSH; only the install needs elevation. (It cannot refresh
#     its source index unprivileged, so results are cached — say so in the digest.)
#   · a CLI that looks absent may just not be on the non-interactive PATH.
#     Synology's `synopkg` lives under /usr/syno/bin/ and is easily missed.
#     Prefer its per-package read-only query; some "check everything" subcommands
#     will also ACT on what they find.

always-on | local           | macos    | brew,npm,softwareupdate,uvtool,pipx
laptop    | <laptop-host>   | macos    | brew,npm,softwareupdate
gateway   | <gateway-host>  | linux    | apt
windows   | <windows-host>  | windows  | manual
nas       | <nas-host>      | synology | manual
